III. Mao Zedong and the Idea of Accelerated Transformation

Mao Zedong’s economic philosophy emerged from the Chinese Communist Revolution and the challenge of transforming a predominantly rural society.

Mao believed that mass political mobilization could accelerate economic and social change. Rather than treating industrialization as a process that necessarily required generations, he sought to compress development into a much shorter period.

This ambition was expressed through the Great Leap Forward.

The promise

The campaign attempted to mobilize rural labor, reorganize agricultural production, expand industrial output, and accelerate the transition toward socialism.

Its underlying ambition was to overcome the perceived limitations of gradual economic development.

Mao’s approach rested on the belief that political organization and collective effort could unlock productive capacities that conventional economic calculations underestimated.

There is an important insight to examine here: economic transformation is not merely a question of purchasing machines. It also requires building institutions, coordinating investment, developing skills, and persuading millions of people to participate in new productive activities.

The catastrophe

The Great Leap Forward also demonstrated the dangers of confusing political enthusiasm with economic productivity.

Unrealistic production targets encouraged false reporting. Coercive agricultural policies undermined rural livelihoods. Political pressure discouraged honest criticism, while resource allocation and procurement decisions contributed to severe food shortages.

The resulting famine killed tens of millions of people, although estimates vary considerably. The historical literature identifies the campaign’s policies and political decisions as central causes of the catastrophe. 

This experience offers a warning for any government contemplating rapid economic transformation.

A policy cannot be considered successful simply because officials report higher production, announce ambitious targets, or mobilize large numbers of workers.

It must be evaluated through actual output, household welfare, productivity, and human survival.

The West African adaptation of Mao’s ambition must therefore begin by rejecting Mao’s most destructive methods.

Its objective should be to accelerate economic learning, technological adoption, and productive investment while protecting individual rights, food security, and the freedom to criticize government policy.

