V. The Continental Market: West Africa as an Economic System

A major obstacle to industrial development is the fragmentation of markets into separate national economies.

Individual countries may lack sufficient domestic demand to support competitive production in certain capital-intensive industries.

Regional integration can help address this limitation.

The Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS) provides an institutional framework for cooperation, although implementation remains a continuing challenge.

A West African Great Leap Forward would seek to strengthen practical economic integration through five initiatives.

First, a more integrated food market. Agricultural products should be able to move efficiently between areas of surplus and areas experiencing shortages.

Second, regional industrial supply chains. Manufacturers should be able to source inputs, components, and services from neighboring countries.

Third, interoperable transportation systems. Ports, roads, railways, and freight networks should operate as complementary elements of a regional logistics system.

Fourth, improved business mobility. Entrepreneurs, skilled workers, and investors should face fewer unnecessary administrative barriers when conducting legitimate business across borders.

Fifth, coordinated technical standards. Shared product standards and certification procedures could reduce duplication and facilitate regional commerce.

The World Bank’s analysis of West African food markets illustrates why this matters: national policies that restrict cross-border trade can undermine the benefits of regional agricultural production and prevent consumers from accessing available supplies. 

The underlying economic principle is straightforward.

A regional market can support a greater diversity of specialized businesses than many isolated national markets.

However, integration must be accompanied by fair competition, consumer protection, transparent regulation, and mechanisms for helping communities that experience concentrated economic losses.

