VI. Burkina Faso and the Possibility of a Sahelian Industrial Transformation

The history of West Africa’s Great Leap Forward would have a particularly important dimension in the Sahel.

Countries such as Burkina Faso face distinctive challenges involving agricultural productivity, climate variability, infrastructure, access to finance, and security.

Yet the region’s development possibilities should not be reduced to its constraints.

Burkina Faso could pursue an industrial strategy centered on agricultural processing, improved energy access, construction materials, mining-related services, digital enterprises, and technical education.

The objective would be to create stronger connections between local production and higher-value economic activity.

Consider the agricultural sector.

Rather than exporting agricultural commodities without substantial domestic processing, businesses could develop more sophisticated capabilities in food preservation, packaging, milling, storage, and distribution.

These activities could create additional employment while connecting rural producers with urban consumers and regional markets.

Likewise, improved technical training could support businesses that maintain agricultural equipment, install solar systems, repair industrial machinery, and provide digital services.

Such development would require investment, reliable infrastructure, functioning institutions, and sustained improvements in security.

It would also require avoiding the mistake of assuming that ambitious industrial targets can substitute for practical economic conditions.

A successful Sahelian development strategy would need to measure its progress through improvements in productivity, household incomes, access to electricity, educational attainment, and the reliability of food supplies.

The purpose would be to make economic opportunity more widely available without requiring people to sacrifice their rights or livelihoods in pursuit of national development targets.

