VII. Mathematical Futurology: Measuring the Great Leap Forward

A credible development program must translate ambition into measurable outcomes.

This is where mathematical modeling can help.

Instead of defining success through slogans, governments could establish public targets for productivity, industrial output, employment, and household welfare.

Consider a simplified model of economic output:

Y_t=A_tK_t^\alpha L_t^\beta H_t^\gamma

Where:

* Y_t represents economic output at time t.
* A_t represents technological and organizational efficiency.
* K_t represents productive capital.
* L_t represents labor.
* H_t represents human capital.
* \alpha,\beta,\gamma represent the contributions of these inputs.

This is an illustrative production function, not a complete description of West African economies.

It highlights an important principle: growth can come from investing in machinery and infrastructure, improving education, expanding productive employment, and using existing resources more efficiently.

A government that increases capital investment without improving project selection may waste scarce resources.

A government that expands education without creating productive employment may struggle to translate educational attainment into higher incomes.

A government that increases agricultural output without improving storage and distribution may fail to deliver corresponding gains in food security.

The challenge is to coordinate these investments.

The compounding effect of economic growth

Suppose a hypothetical economy achieves sustained real GDP per capita growth of 5% annually.

The time required for its real GDP per capita to double can be approximated using the rule of 72:

T\approx\frac{72}{g}

Where g is the annual growth rate expressed as a percentage.

At 5% annual growth:

T\approx\frac{72}{5}=14.4\text{ years}

This means that sustained growth at that rate could approximately double real GDP per capita in fourteen to fifteen years.

The calculation is illustrative. It does not predict that any particular West African country will achieve this rate, and GDP per capita does not capture the distribution of income or the full quality of life.

Nevertheless, it demonstrates why sustained productivity improvements matter.

The objective of the Great Leap Forward would be to improve the conditions that make long-term growth possible, not to promise extraordinary growth regardless of economic fundamentals.

A public development dashboard

A regional development initiative could monitor:

Indicator	What it measures
Real GDP per capita	Growth in average economic output per person.
Agricultural yield	Productivity of agricultural land.
Manufacturing value added	Expansion of domestic industrial production.
Electricity reliability	Consistency of access to usable power.
Labor productivity	Output produced per worker or hour worked.
Food insecurity	Households’ ability to obtain sufficient food.
School completion	Progress in foundational education.
Technical employment	Growth in skilled and industrial occupations.
Extreme poverty	Changes in severe material deprivation.
Business formation and survival	Growth and sustainability of productive enterprises.

The dashboard should be publicly available and independently audited.

It should also report negative outcomes, including rising food insecurity, excessive debt, environmental damage, and displacement.

The most important difference between a responsible development campaign and a disastrous one is whether institutions are allowed to report failure honestly and change course.

