IX. The Historical Meaning of West Africa’s Great Leap Forward

The phrase Great Leap Forward ordinarily evokes the Chinese campaign of 1958–1962.

For West Africa, it can instead serve as a framework for imagining a different kind of historical turning point.

Such a turning point would occur when the region begins to systematically convert its agricultural resources, mineral wealth, human capital, entrepreneurial activity, and regional markets into increasingly sophisticated productive capabilities.

Its success would not depend on one leader, one ideology, or one centrally directed campaign.

It would depend on whether millions of people can participate in a growing economy that rewards innovation, supports productive investment, and creates opportunities across national boundaries.

The transformation would be cumulative.

Farmers would gain access to better technology and markets. Manufacturers would develop new products. Universities would train more engineers and researchers. Entrepreneurs would build businesses that serve regional customers. Governments would invest in infrastructure and public services. Regional institutions would reduce unnecessary barriers to trade.

Over time, these changes could reinforce one another.

The resulting economic transformation would not happen overnight, and it would not proceed at the same speed in every country.

But sustained improvements in productivity and institutional capacity could change the region’s long-term development trajectory.

Conclusion: A Leap Forward Without a Leap Into Catastrophe

The historical significance of Mao Zedong’s Great Leap Forward lies partly in the enormous ambition it expressed and partly in the devastating consequences of its implementation.

It demonstrated that governments can mobilize societies around transformative objectives, but also that political determination cannot abolish economic constraints.

West Africa faces a different historical situation and has the opportunity to draw lessons from that experience.

Its development challenge is not simply to reproduce China’s institutions, copy its political history, or accelerate every economic activity simultaneously.

It is to identify the investments, institutions, technologies, and regional partnerships that can produce sustained improvements in human welfare.

The region’s next great transformation would require agricultural modernization, industrial diversification, technical education, reliable infrastructure, scientific research, and effective regional integration.

It would also require democratic accountability, freedom of inquiry, protection of individual rights, and the ability to correct failed policies.

The ultimate objective should not be to make West Africa appear industrialized in official statistics.

It should be to create societies in which people can live longer, eat better, learn more, earn higher incomes, establish successful businesses, and participate meaningfully in their political and economic futures.

If the Chinese Great Leap Forward attempted to compress industrialization through mass mobilization, West Africa’s alternative should seek to accelerate development through knowledge, productive investment, regional cooperation, and human freedom.

That would be the defining promise of West Africa’s Great Leap Forward: not economic transformation at any cost, but economic transformation that makes prosperity more attainable for the people it is intended to serve.

